AN  - rayyan-603929593
TI  - SPERM HEAD SEGMENTATION IN MICROSCOPIC IMAGES USING A U-NET MODEL
Y1  - 2026
T2  - Актуальні проблеми сучасної медицини: Вісник Української медичної стоматологічної академії
VL  - 26
IS  - 2
SP  - 125-130
AU  - Hula, I. D.
AB  - <jats:p>Male factor infertility remains one of the leading causes of impaired reproductive function, and morphological assessment of spermatozoa, particularly the sperm head, is an important component of standard semen analysis. At the same time, manual morphological assessment remains labor-intensive, subjective, and highly dependent on the examiner’s experience, which creates a need for automated approaches to the analysis of microscopic images. Objective. To develop and evaluate the performance of a deep learning model for automated sperm head segmentation in microscopic images.     Materials and methods. The study included microscopic images of individual spermatozoa obtained from semen smears of 70 patients aged 25–45 years after standard cytological staining. Manual annotation of sperm head contours was performed in QuPath. To create the training dataset, both morphologically normal and pathologically altered spermatozoa were manually annotated, including normal, tapered, pyriform, amorphous, and small or microcephalic sperm heads. Segmentation was performed using a U-Net model configured according to a previously validated setup, with encoder depth 5, ReLU activation function, and RMSprop optimizer. Segmentation quality was assessed using the Dice coefficient and Intersection over Union metrics, as well as by visual expert review.     Results. The developed model demonstrated an overall high level of segmentation across all studied morphological classes. The highest Dice coefficient was recorded for pyriform forms, 0.955 ± 0.015, while high values were also obtained for normal forms, 0.942 ± 0.012. For tapered and amorphous forms, the Dice coefficient was 0.912 ± 0.011 and 0.920 ± 0.013, respectively. The highest IoU value was observed for tapered forms, 0.890 ± 0.010. The lowest values were found for small or microcephalic sperm heads, with a Dice coefficient of 0.890 ± 0.010 and an IoU of 0.840 ± 0.013; however, segmentation quality remained high in this group as well. Low standard deviation values across all classes indicated the relative stability of the model performance.     Conclusion. Thus, the U-Net based model provides high-quality automated sperm head segmentation in microscopic images and may be considered a reliable basis for further automation of sperm morphological assessment, particularly for morphometric analysis and subsequent recognition of pathological forms.</jats:p>
